 "result": "`test_discount_rounding` passes now. I ran `python -m unittest -v tests.test_pricing` and both tests in the file passed (`Ran 2 tests ... OK`). pytest isn't installed here, so I used unittest.\n\n**The bug:** `apply_discount` in `calc/pricing.py` converted to cents with `int(...)`, which cuts off the decimals instead of rounding. Floating-point math makes `2.30 * 0.5 * 100` come out as `114.99999999999999`. `int()` turned that into `114`, so the function returned `1.14` instead of `1.15`.\n\n**The fix:** one line, using `round(...)` in place of `int(...)`:\n\n```python\nreturn round(price * (1 - pct / 100) * 100) / 100\n```\n\nThis changes behaviour beyond the failing case: discounted prices are now rounded to the nearest cent rather than always rounded down. That seems to be the intent of \"in cents (two places)\" and of the test. Python's `round()` rounds exact half-cent ties to the even cent, so if you need half-cents to always round up, use `decimal`.\n\nI didn't run the other test files (`test_parse.py`, `test_many.py`).",
